      "why_read": "Understand why modern distributed systems prematurely dismissed quorum replication in favor of Raft. You will gain a clear perspective on real-world replication trade-offs across industry architectures.",
      "hn_id": "50511670",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50511670",
      "image": null,
      "content": "The database industry has developed an unhealthy obsession with Raft at the expense of quorum replication.\n\nNearly every major distributed database developed over the last decade defaults to consensus algorithms such as Raft or Multi-Paxos. Meanwhile, pioneering quorum systems like Riak, Voldemort, and Cassandra have either been phased out, replaced, or relegated to legacy infrastructure. Consensus has become the default answer to every replication problem.\n\nThis consensus monoculture creates significant operational overhead. Leader-based consensus protocols require lease management, introduce latency spikes during leader elections, and force all writes through a single bottleneck. Quorum replication trades linearizability for continuous availability and simpler decentralized write paths. For many read-heavy or multi-region workloads, majority quorums deliver higher resiliency against network partitions without requiring complex leader election mechanics.\n\nModern hardware, faster networks, and hybrid logical clocks eliminate many of the historical consistency headaches that doomed early Dynamo implementations. Reconsidering quorum protocols can unlock simpler storage engines with lower tail latencies.\n\nRaft is a formidable tool, but not every storage system requires a leader."
    },
    {
