      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50509129",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Random fuzzing often hits diminishing returns when testing complex parsers and stateful slices. A practical alternative pairs compiler internals with SMT solvers: inspect Go machine code for explicit bounds-check traps that survived the compiler optimizer, then have an LLM translate those code paths into formal Z3 constraint rules.\n\nFuzzers generate inputs by mutating existing seeds, meaning deeply nested edge cases with narrow satisfy conditions can remain hidden indefinitely. By contrast, compiling down to assembly exposes exactly which bounds checks were not proven safe by the compiler. Using Gemini to formulate the solver model lets Z3 synthesize exact byte sequences that force an execution down an unhandled panic path.\n\nIn testing against Markdown linters, this pipeline caught three planted bugs and uncovered three latent panics that traditional fuzz testing missed completely. Combining formal solvers with LLM-based translation turns compiler trap analysis into concrete, reproducible test suites.\n\nConstraint solvers provide mathematical certainty where fuzzing relies entirely on luck."
    },
