      "content": "Automating web browsers with vision-language models often feels like using a sledgehammer on a thumbtack. Deploying a 7-billion-parameter multimodal model just to locate an input field or button consumes gigabytes of VRAM and introduces frustrating selector hallucinations.\n\nFolate solves this by ditching generative models for a lightweight discriminative cross-encoder. Fine-tuned from an Electra backbone on Mind2Web and quantized to an INT8 ONNX runtime, the model occupies only 107 megabytes on disk and runs comfortably in CPU memory under 250 megabytes.\n\nInstead of predicting arbitrary coordinates or hallucinating XPath strings, Folate extracts real candidate nodes directly from the DOM using lxml and scores pairs against the natural language target. This guarantees that every output corresponds to an actual interactive element while matching or exceeding the accuracy of models sixty times its size.\n\nSpecialized discriminative rankers routinely outperform massive generative models when the search space can be explicitly enumerated."
