      "content": "Distributed transactions across microservices fail in complicated ways, particularly when external network calls fail midway through a long process. Building ad-hoc retry mechanisms often introduces duplicate side effects and state corruption.\n\nFCQRS.Workflows tackles this problem by applying event sourcing directly to step orchestration within .NET. Instead of running ephemeral procedural steps, the workflow engine records the output of every external interaction and decision point as an event. When a host node dies mid-stream, the runtime resumes execution exactly at the failed step, reading preceding inputs from the event store without re-executing completed operations.\n\nThis pattern separates orchestration logic from sagas, eliminating brittle state machine handoffs. The result is deterministic recovery for long-running transactions while maintaining strict idempotency guarantees across unreliable third-party integrations.\n\nDesigning resilience into distributed systems begins by treating execution steps as immutable state transitions rather than transient code paths."
