      "content": "Scaling Mixture-of-Experts models to long contexts typically hits a wall not from average GPU memory usage, but from unpredictable peak allocations. Standard parallelism schedules leave four critical peaks completely unbounded: expert dispatch, vocabulary projection, gradient checkpoint boundaries, and optimizer state.\n\nLowering just one bottleneck does not solve the problem because the others simply become the next failure point at scale. This paper introduces fixed-size GPU working set schedules that address all four peaks simultaneously without changing loss or gradient accuracy.\n\nThe system bounds expert dispatch with capped source contributions (PipelinedLLEP) and circulates activations or weights in a ring at the vocabulary projection (Ring-DTP) while streaming logits into an online log-sum-exp. It pairs these with selective checkpoint offloading for long-lived boundary tensors and a bucket-pipelined AdamW CPU offload schedule.\n\nDeterministic peak bounds beat reactive memory management every time you scale batch size or context length."
