      "content": "Serialization formats usually force a strict trade-off: row formats like Protobuf offer simple record streaming but struggle with compression on large arrays, while pure columnar formats like Parquet require heavy batching and schema overhead.\n\ncolbin introduces a hybrid row-columnar binary serialization format implemented in Go, Rust, and JavaScript that eliminates schema compilation entirely. Messages are encoded in a row format with a single byte header per field, but when collections reach eight or more records, colbin transparently switches to columnar layout using bit-packing transforms.\n\nBecause this format decision happens dynamically per value during encoding, a tiny three-field message and a million-row tabular dataset share the same binary specification. It delivers smaller payloads and faster throughput than Protocol Buffers without requiring any code generation steps or external dependencies.\n\nDynamically adapting between row-oriented records and columnar vectors provides a compelling blueprint for next-generation distributed wire protocols."
