      "content": "Migrating a monorepo containing over one million lines of active code from Flow to TypeScript is notoriously dangerous. The usual trap is inserting thousands of type suppression comments or temporarily pausing type checking, which permanently destroys developer trust in the toolchain.\n\nYelp Engineering bypassed this pitfall by refusing to execute either a massive holiday-break rewrite or a disruptive repository freeze. Instead, they designed an incremental migration pipeline that preserved strict type integrity at every boundary between both type checkers.\n\nMaintaining bidirectional safety while hundreds of engineers ship daily commits requires rigid boundaries and automated conversion validation. When teams attempt large-scale migrations by loosening compiler constraints, those suppressions rarely get cleaned up, leaving invisible type holes behind for years.\n\nGradual migrations only succeed when the intermediary steps are as strictly enforced as the final destination."
