      "authors": [
        "ersinesen"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how address hash collisions in CPU cache way predictors bypass isolation boundaries. You will learn the mechanics behind sampling data across confidential virtual machines, microcode, and system management mode.",
      "hn_id": "50505500",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50505500",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Hardware isolation boundaries often look rock-solid until microarchitectural optimization shortcuts get exposed. The SKYFALL attack (CVE-2026-40671) demonstrates how transient execution on AMD processors can bypass SEV-SNP guest boundaries, recover microcode patches, and leak System Management Mode data.\n\nThe vulnerability stems from how AMD CPUs accelerate L1 data cache way prediction. To avoid inspecting every cache way while waiting for virtual-to-physical address translation via the TLB, the core predicts the correct way using a small address hash known as a micro-tag. The critical flaw is that this predictor hashes virtual, physical, and CPU-internal addresses indiscriminately.\n\nAn attacker can engineer collisions between accessible virtual addresses and privileged physical addresses. When combined with gaps in existing Transient Scheduler Attack mitigations, the CPU speculatively loads and samples architecturally inaccessible data across isolation rings.\n\nFor systems engineers designing confidential compute runtimes, this highlights the recurring trade-off between speculative execution performance and hardware-enforced tenant isolation."
