        "Luka Ribar"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how researchers compressed an 11B vision-language model into under 4 GB to achieve local execution on mobile CPUs through custom format codesign and quantization-aware training.",
      "hn_id": "50505240",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50505240",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Running vision-language models on the edge usually requires severe compromises on model scale, but custom numeric formats change that calculus. Graphcore and Arm compressed Llama 3.2 Vision Instruct (11B parameters) from over 21 GB down to just 3.7 GB, allowing it to execute entirely on an Android mobile CPU.\n\nThe implementation packs three weights into a single byte using a novel representation co-designed for Arm microarchitectures. Rather than relying on simple post-training quantization, the team utilized quantization-aware training over diverse prompts to preserve perceptual fidelity and instruction-following capability.\n\nMemory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck on mobile runtimes. Shrinking the memory footprint below the device budget of 4 GB transforms inference from an impossible I/O thrashing problem into a manageable compute workload directly on the CPU.\n\nOptimizing edge deployments increasingly depends on tight co-design between model weight quantization and hardware-level memory architectures."
