      "authors": [
        "amirkabbara"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to learn how Papr balances high retrieval accuracy with lower token usage on the LongMemEval benchmark. You will discover how efficient context selection allows smaller models to achieve results competitive with much larger systems.",
      "hn_id": "50504245",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50504245",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Feeding an entire conversation history into an LLM context window is often both wasteful and inaccurate. On the LongMemEval benchmark, dumping the full 112,000 tokens of chat history directly into GPT-4o yields an accuracy of only 60.2 percent.\n\nRecent benchmark results from Papr demonstrate how targeted retrieval outclasses brute force context stuffing. By pairing selective retrieval with a reranking stage, their system achieved a 96.0 percent score while sending only 16.5k tokens to the answer model per question. That represents a fraction of the context window used by competing systems, which often load 43k to 60k tokens.\n\nEven more notable for production engineering costs, pairing this retrieval strategy with a compact model like GPT-5-mini achieved 94.4 percent accuracy. That rivals setups running frontier models at a fraction of the inference latency and bill.\n\nEffective memory in AI agents is fundamentally an information retrieval problem, not a context length competition."
