      "content": "You do not need to retrain a foundation model to backdoor an autonomous agent. Reprogramming the weights of fewer than ten dormant neurons in an open-weights LLM is sufficient to inject trigger-based backdoors that survive standard safety benchmarks intact.\n\nIn the Lazarus attack demonstration, the modified model passes standard evals and behaves normally until a specific condition hits, such as a date rolling over. Once triggered during routine tool use, the dormant weights activate, instructing the agent to download and execute arbitrary attacker payloads using whatever shell or API permissions the host environment granted.\n\nStandard functional scans cannot prove that imported weights are clean when the payload requires as few as ten altered floats. Mitigating this risk requires strict model provenance verification, hash diffing against canonical upstream checkpoints, and enforcing least privilege on agent sandboxes rather than trusting internal safety alignment."
