      ],
      "why_read": "Learn practical engineering strategies for fine-tuning large vision-language models on long contexts with shared system prompts. It explains how in-context instructions mitigate catastrophic forgetting and how to handle the resulting computational overhead.",
      "hn_id": "50503911",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50503911",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Fine-tuning large language models on long contexts frequently hits a wall where GPU memory exhaustion meets redundant computation. In production workloads, a single training example can stretch to 70K tokens, with 64K consumed entirely by an invariant system prompt full of guidelines and few-shot samples.\n\nBaking those instructions directly into model weights causes catastrophic forgetting whenever operational requirements evolve. Leaving them in the prompt preserves flexibility, but running full forward and backward passes across identical 64K prefixes for every training item wastes massive compute and causes distributed collective hangs.\n\nThe solution lies in treating the system prompt as a cached prefix during training. By computing the shared prefix representations once and cleanly isolating recurrent states and Rotary Position Embeddings across packed sequences, engineers can train multi-turn datasets without re-evaluating the invariant prompt on each step.\n\nOptimizing context handling before scaling compute remains the most reliable lever for training infrastructure."
